<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Corieltauvi</h1><p class="st-meta">50 BCE – 47 CE (approx.) · capital Ratae Corieltauvorum (Leicester)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">East Midlands people who issued coins under paired rulers and buried the Hallaton Treasure, and who passed under Roman rule largely without resistance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Corieltauvi occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicestershire">Leicestershire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a> and parts of the surrounding counties in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>; the older name Coritani was corrected after an inscription naming them was found. They grew from local farming communities on the river valleys and wolds, with major centres at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, Old Sleaford and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>From the late 1st century BCE they issued gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins, often with two names together, such as VEP CORF or AVN COST, which may point to joint rule; later issues name Volisios and Dumnocoveros. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallaton-treasure">Hallaton Treasure</a>, a ritual hoard of more than 5,000 coins and a Roman cavalry helmet, shows close contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> before the conquest. Pottery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-working">iron-working</a> flourished.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The region was occupied soon after 43, apparently with little fighting, and by the late 40s Roman forts held the area; the legio IX was later based at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln</a>. The people formed the civitas of the Corieltauvi, governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratae-corieltauvorum">Ratae Corieltauvorum</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>, within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/corieltauvi.jpg" alt="Corieltauvi" width="320" height="453"><figcaption>Image: Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Dobunni.svg: *Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Cornovii_(Midlands).svg: *Map_of_the_Territory_... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Territory_of_the_Corieltauvi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>50 BCE – 47 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ratae Corieltauvorum (Leicester)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Volisios, Dumnocoveros</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Common Brittonic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Coritani</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-2&amp;focus=corieltauvi" aria-label="Corieltauvi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4654 363 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Corieltauvi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4974 619L5304 942M4974 619L5020 640"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5020 640h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4974 619h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4974 619h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Ratae Corieltauvorum. <a href="/atlas?year=-2&amp;focus=corieltauvi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
